      "The room was small and low, tucked under the slope of the roof, with a single bed and a rag rug and a window the size of a tea tray that looked directly out over the harbour. In summer, as a child, she had lain here with the window wedged open on a ruler and listened to the halyards ringing against the masts, a thousand tiny bells, and the chimney humming behind the wall. Someone had made up the bed. Not recently, she thought, there was dust on the pillow, but with care, the sheet turned down in a neat triangle and the blue wool blanket folded across the foot with its edges squared. Her grandfather had never been a man for hospitality in words. He had made beds instead.",
