      "They did not go far. The back door of the house opened onto a narrow stone yard, and the yard onto a flight of nine worn steps that went down to the quay, and it was on the sixth step that they sat, in the lee of the wall, with a plastic bag over Tomi’s knees and the letter between them. The rain had stopped. In the stillness after it the whole harbour seemed to have drawn breath. The tide was far out, and the mud shone under the lamps like the back of some enormous sleeping animal. Boats lay tilted on their keels in the shallows, patient, the way boats are when the sea has stepped out for a moment and promised to come back. The smell of weed and diesel and wet rope was so strong and so familiar that Nia could almost see her grandfather in it, bent over a lobster pot with his pipe in his teeth, humming something tuneless and cheerful that she had never been able to name.",
